    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d289">

                <pb facs="0372" n="362" sameAs="frus:frus1943China/pg362"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/15150: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gauss</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1943-10-20T09:00:00+07:00">October 20, 1943—9
                            a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 3:20 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1982. Embassy’s despatch 1580, September 16.<anchor n="71" xml:id="d289-f1"/>
                    Telegram of October 15 from Clubb at Tihwa states native disturbances reported
                    in Altai region and south Sinkiang and Turki language circulars said recently
                        <pb facs="0373" n="363" xml:id="pg363"/> distributed in Tihwa calling on
                    people to rise against Sheng’s rule.</p>
                <p>Clubb reports (<hi rendition="#italic">a</hi>) that Chinese officials reputedly
                    believe disturbances are instigated by Soviets, but he believes unsettled
                    political grievances of non-Chinese population and continuing economic
                    deterioration are important factors in this situation.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gauss</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
